  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 353 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nTonight:\nScattered thunderstorms continue to fester across eastern\nMinnesota & western Wisconsin this afternoon, with brief\ninstances of small hail occurring in the strongest storms. This\ncontinued activity through the day has basically mitigated our\npotential for explosive storm growth this afternoon, despite\nvery steep mid-level lapse rates & surface based CAPE values in\nexcess of 3000 J/kg.\n\nOur eyes continue to be on the complex of thunderstorms exiting\neastern North Dakota into northwestern Minnesota, & especially \nwhere it tracks through the evening. So far, the thunderstorm\ncomplex has been making an orderly progression off to the east,\nwith no signs of a southeasterly turn towards our area of\ncentral Minnesota. Recent radar trends suggest the line of\nstorms may even be breaking up into discrete clusters or even\ndiscrete supercells. These would be even less likely to migrate\nsoutheastwards, so we will continue to wait & see if any\nthunderstorm complexes can develop back to the west across\neastern ND, or something new can develop from the remnants of\nthe ongoing storms. It's still too early to write of any severe\npotential for tonight given the environment in place & \ninstability gradient extending southeastwards into north- \ncentral Minnesota, & northwest Wisconsin, although our\nconfidence is increasing that it should remain north of an \nAlexandria-St. Cloud-Rice Rake/Barron line. Damaging wind gusts\nwould be the primary hazard with this thunderstorm complex, \nfollowed by large hail. Timing looks to mainly be tonight - 7-9 \nPM across western portions of central MN, 9-11 PM across eastern\nportions, & 11 PM-1 AM across northwest Wisconsin.\n\nFriday:\nFriday will be dry, with a low chance of some isolated\nthunderstorms developing near the Iowa-Minnesota border tomorrow\nafternoon. Should these storms develop, the strongest storms\ncould contain large hail. Otherwise, heat & humidity will be \nthe main weather concern with temperatures expected to warm into\nthe low 90s across much of the area. Less confident is the dew \npoint forecast, with many of the latest 18Z high-resolutin \nmodels suggesting we have some drier air move into at least \ncentral Minnesota 7 western Wisconsin tomorrow afternoon. This \ncould create a sharp gradient where heat index values stay in \nthe low 90s across central MN & western WI, while approaching \n100 degrees across far-southern Minnesota. We only look to \napproach Heat Advisory criteria in the twin Cities metro (where \nthresholds are at 95 degrees), but have decided to hold off on \nany issuance at this time given the trend towards lower dew \npoints & heat index values tomorrow afternoon.\n\nThis Weekend:\nLow confidence on the heat forecast this weekend, owing to the\npossibility for cooler flow off of Lake Superior saturday &\nprecipitation/cloud cover Sunday. We could see another day of\nheat & humidity Saturday with many of the legacy global models\nsuggesting highs around 90 along with muggy dew points, however\nAI models & a few of the high-resolution models suggest we could\nbe entrenched in cooler northeast flow off of Lake Superior\nthroughout the day. This would result in a rather comfortable\nday with highs around 80 and less muggy dewpoints, while a\ngradient sets up somewhere over western Minnesota where we still \nsee heat index values approach 100 degrees where the lake- \ncooled air does not reach.\n\nA shortwave arrives late Saturday night, providing another\nchance for scattered showers & thunderstorms overnight Saturday\nnight through Sunday morning. Low confidence on any of the \ndetails, but the best chances for appreciable rainfall appear to\nbe across western Minnesota.\n\nNext Week:\nThe upper air pattern remains active as a number of disturbances\nride over the ridge centered over the region. Confidence is low\non when & where the greatest precipitation chances will be, the\nmain window for more widespread rainfall looks to be late Monday\nthrough Tuesday. Temperatures will depend on the timing of any\nprecipitation systems, but will tend to be well-above normal\nearlier in the week with some cooling relief (but still likely\nabove- normal) coming later in the week. For reference - average\ntemperatures for early September are in the mid to upper 70s for\nhighs & upper 50s for lows.",
